// OsImportSlot — the OS primitives a dynamically-linked target reaches through an import
// table, and the slot index a call site records for its writer to patch.
//
// ⭐ THE SLOT-INDEX MODEL IS OS-NEUTRAL; ONLY THE SYMBOL SPELLING IS NOT. A call site emits
// "call through import slot k" and the object writer resolves k to an address: the Mach-O
// writer against a `__got` entry dyld binds, the PE writer against an IAT entry (`IatSlot`,
// the identical shape). Nothing about `k` is Mach-O, which is why this enum does NOT belong
// in `Targets/Macos/` — two files under `Targets/Arm64/` (the isel and the entry stub) named
// a Mach-O type to say "the exit primitive", a layering leak that only became visible when
// arm64-linux arrived and needed the same three primitives with NO import table at all.
//
// The per-format SYMBOL map stays with its writer (`machoImportSymbol` renders the Mach-O
// ABI's leading underscore: a C `exit` is the symbol `_exit`), because that IS format
// knowledge. Adding an import is still: append a case here and its symbol there.
//
// ⚠ arm64-LINUX USES NONE OF THIS. Linux permits raw syscalls, so `osExit`/`osWriteStdout`/
// `osAllocPages` lower to `svc #0` and the ELF image carries no import table, no GOT and no
// dynamic linker (see `Arm64Runtime.arm64OsCallKind`). The enum is reached only on the
// import-table route.
//
// `exit` terminates the process with main's return code. `write` is both the panic runtime's
// route to stderr and the `osWriteStdout`/`print` primitive. `mmap` is the page source
// `osAllocPages` bottoms out in; MAP_ANON returns ZEROED pages, satisfying the slab's zeroing
// contract. `munmap` releases such a mapping (`osFreePages`) and `madvise` drops its physical
// backing while KEEPING the mapping (`osDecommitPages`).
//
// ⛔ **THIS LIST SAID "No `munmap`: the slab never decommits, so a page-free import would be dead",
// AND THAT SENTENCE OUTLIVED ITS PREMISE.** It was true of the bump heap, which never frees; it was
// already false for the green-thread scheduler, which releases a completed GT's stack — that op just
// happened to be x64-windows-gated, so the missing import surfaced as a PANIC in the arm64 isel rather
// than as a link error here. ⚠ Only arm64-macOS actually reaches these two: the two Linux lanes make the
// same calls as raw `svc`/`syscall` and need no import table at all (see the ⚠ note above), which is why
// two names appear here for four lanes.
// ⚠ APPENDED, never inserted: `osImportSlotIndex` is the enum ORDINAL and every emitted `arm64ImportCall`
// records it, so a reorder would silently repoint every existing call site.
//
// ⚠ There is no `mprotect` slot, and that is not an omission: POSIX has no COMMIT state to change a
// protection into, so `osCommitPages` is an `mmap` of the sub-range (`MAP_FIXED`) and reaches the `mmap`
// slot already listed — exactly as `osReservePages` does. One page source, three spellings of its arguments:
// allocate, reserve, commit.
export enum OsImportSlot
	exit
	write
	mmap
	munmap
	madvise
	// --- the arm64-macOS host surface (`Targets/Arm64/Arm64DarwinRuntime`) ---
	//
	// ⚠ **THESE ARE REACHED FROM ONE LANE ONLY, AND THE ENUM IS STILL THE RIGHT HOME.** Every slot above is
	// reached by arm64-macOS alone too (the Linux lanes make the same calls as raw `svc`), so "only one lane
	// uses it" was never this list's membership rule — being an OS primitive reached through an IMPORT
	// TABLE is. What a Linux lane needs is a syscall NUMBER, which is a different fact in a different file.
	//
	// `clock_gettime_nsec_np` answers a Darwin clock in nanoseconds directly in x0 — the calendar, the
	// monotonic counter and this thread's CPU time are one import asked with three different `clockid_t`s,
	// which is why three facilities cost one slot. `nanosleep` is the timed park.
	clockGettimeNsecNp
	nanosleep
	// `sysconf` answers a numbered system parameter in x0 — `osCpuCount` asks it
	// `_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN`. ⚠ **IT IS THE FIRST SLOT HERE WITH NO LINUX TWIN AT ALL**, where every slot
	// above merely reaches its twin as a raw `svc`: Linux serves this parameter through a libc FUNCTION and
	// numbers it differently, so there is no syscall number to pair with this slot even in principle
	// (`StdToArm64Conversion.DarwinScNprocessorsOnln` carries the numbers and their provenance — do not
	// restate them here). That is why `targetProvidesFacility` gives `cpuCount` to arm64-macOS alone, and why
	// `lowerOsCpuCount` asks `arm64OsCallKind` before emitting a call through this slot.
	sysconf
end 'OsImportSlot'

// The import slot index a call site records and the writer patches against. One accessor
// over the enum, exactly like `iatSlotIndex`.
export function osImportSlotIndex(slot OsImportSlot) returns RelocSlotIndex
	return slot.ordinal as RelocSlotIndex
end 'osImportSlotIndex'
